<Book title="Coverage and editorial audit"><span class="eyebrow">VERIFIED 2 NOVEMBER 2026</span><h1>Coverage, without overclaiming</h1><p class="dek">The required 61 topics are the minimum map. A page's existence is not evidence that its explanation is finished.</p><div class="metrics">{Object.entries(n).map(([status,count])=><div><strong>{count}</strong><span>{status==='complete'?'Complete authored chapters':status==='reviewed'?'Independently reviewed':status}</span></div>)}</div>
<div class="prose"><h2>Scope criteria</h2><p>Include major mechanisms with distinct inputs, transformations, output contracts, or important applications: oracle search; Fourier and hidden structure; estimation; simulation; matrix transformations; walks; variational and adiabatic algorithms; learning and sampling; specialized number theory and topology. The catalog is broad, not exhaustive over every published variant.</p><p>The <a href="https://quantumalgorithmzoo.org/">Quantum Algorithm Zoo</a> was used to discover families, and the <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03011">end-to-end survey by Dalzell and coauthors</a> to audit access and output assumptions. Original papers and official educational sources appear in each lesson. The survey's posted revision describes coverage through mid-2024; its presence does not imply a complete November 2026 literature search.</p><h2>Order and bridges</h2><p>The requested order is preserved: Deutsch first, Grover second, followed by all numbered required topics. Optional definitions and inline bridges supply complex arithmetic, eigensystems, trigonometry, density matrices, reversible arithmetic, and probability at the point of use. Main-path versus branch status is recorded in the manifest. The synthesis follows the 61 required entries.</p><h2>Significant additions audited</h2><p>Developments within established families are integrated where they change a mechanism or resource comparison: variable-time amplification and modern linear solvers; Regev's factoring proposal and its assumptions; interaction-picture simulation; quantum eigenvalue transformation of unitaries; linear combinations of Hamiltonian simulation for differential equations; quantum fast-forwarding of reversible chains; and modern detailed-balance Gibbs samplers. Their presence does not elevate a partial derivation to complete status. Follow the corresponding lesson's exact gaps below.</p><p>State preparation, reversible arithmetic, Hadamard/SWAP tests, and expectation estimation have reusable worked library entries. Teleportation, superdense coding, error correction, and fault tolerance are supporting protocols/engineering topics; they are not counted as missing algorithms or substituted for the required families. Cryptographic protocols, quantum communication complexity, and exhaustive variants of quantum optimization are outside this finite edition.</p><aside class="revision-note context-box" id="revision-2026-11-02"><p><strong>Revision note, 2 November 2026.</strong> Error correction and fault tolerance are now an in-scope module, "FT: Error correction and fault tolerance", reached through the <a href="/path/">decision path</a>. The module is being added in phases, and its lessons appear in this audit as each is published. The scope paragraph above is kept as originally written. Every lesson now also carries one tier label: Decision path, Complete path, or Deferred branch.</p></aside><h2>What the statuses mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Complete:</strong> an authored end-to-end worked lesson with its required sections and exercises. This is an author assessment, not independent peer review.</li><li><strong>Reviewed:</strong> substantive independent review of the explanation, with identified fixes applied. Automated checks alone do not earn this label.</li><li><strong>Partial:</strong> substantive material exists, with the exact remaining teaching or derivation gaps disclosed. It is not a finished tutorial.</li><li><strong>Planned:</strong> metadata only, no tutorial destination. No blank pages are presented as lessons.</li></ul><p>The build validates IDs, references, graph cycles, section content, mathematical rendering, and exercise presence. Those checks cannot prove pedagogical completeness or every complexity theorem. Numerical tests check representative calculations independently of rendered prose. Browser tests check the actual interactions. The repository's validation report records the commands actually run.</p></div>
<h2>Topic-by-topic audit</h2><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Coverage table"><table><thead><tr><th>Lesson</th><th>Type / route</th><th>Editorial state</th><th>Evidence / exact remaining gaps</th></tr></thead><tbody>{lessons.map(l=><tr><td>{l.editorial==='planned'?label(l):<a href={`/lessons/${l.id}/`}>{label(l)}</a>}</td><td>{l.kind}<br/>{l.path}<br/><span class="small">{tierLabels[tier(l)]}</span></td><td>{l.editorial}</td><td><span class="small">{lessonMarkdown(l).split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length.toLocaleString()} source words. {l.gaps.length?l.gaps.join(' '):'Worked example, cost model, explained exercises, and references authored.'}</span></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p><a href="/curriculum.json">Machine-readable curriculum and dependencies</a> · <a href="/references/">Verification details for every source</a></p></Book>
